      "name": "value_for_money",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly redundant with the existing original 'Value For Money' rating column, adding little new axis. Sample 2 labelled fair_reasonable despite no value/price mention is a weakly grounded inference."
    },
    {
      "name": "class_premium_decline",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A relevant flag for the Class dimension of the query, offering a distinct grouping axis. But the true/false judgement (premium experience declining) is often not derivable from short review text and risks confident assertions where the text is silent; groundedness is uncertain."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.68,
  "source_groundedness": 0.75,
  "rationale": "Several added columns (crew_attentiveness, food_quality, aircraft_condition, class_premium_decline) provide genuine thematic axes well suited to relating themes to Overall Rating across traveller type and class. However, seat_comfort, customer_service, and value_for_money substantially restate existing original rating columns or each other, limiting new leverage. Groundedness is generally good with appropriate NULLs for silent rows, but multiple columns over-assert negative/specific labels on rows where the source text is silent (notably the seat-only review in sample 2), indicating a tendency to hallucinate confident values rather than abstain."
